-- Q2442: Tax Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 6212 and Section 6213, the standard IRS dispute resolution sequence is: (1) the IRS issues a 30-day letter proposing adjustments; (2) the taxpayer may appeal to the IRS Appeals Office; (3) if unresolved, the IRS issues a statutory notice of deficiency (90-day letter); (4) the taxpayer may petition Tax Court within 90 days. This allows pre-payment adjudication.
Wrong (A): This reverses the order — the 30-day letter precedes the 90-day letter, not the other way around.
Wrong (B): Appeals comes after the 30-day letter, not before it, and District Court requires prior payment of the deficiency.
Wrong (C): A Tax Court petition follows the 90-day letter; it does not precede the 30-day letter.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2442;

-- Q2444: Tax Procedures [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 6654(d)(1)(B), taxpayers avoid the estimated tax underpayment penalty by paying at least 90% of the current year''s tax or 100% of the prior year''s tax. For taxpayers with prior-year AGI exceeding $150,000, Section 6654(d)(1)(C) raises the prior-year safe harbor to 110%. Henderson''s AGI of $130,000 falls below this threshold.
Wrong (A): The 80% threshold does not appear in the estimated tax penalty provisions; the correct current-year threshold is 90%.
Wrong (B): Paying 100% of the current year''s tax avoids the penalty, but this omits the prior-year safe harbor alternative.
Wrong (D): The 50% threshold has no basis in the estimated tax penalty rules under Section 6654.$EXPL$
