-- Q2459: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under common law contract principles and UCC Section 2-210, contract rights — especially the right to receive payment — are freely assignable unless the contract expressly prohibits assignment or the assignment would materially change the obligor''s duty. Davis''s assignment of the payment right to First Bank is valid.
Wrong (B): Evans''s consent is not required for the assignment of payment rights. The obligor must pay the assignee once notified.
Wrong (C): This confuses assignment of rights with delegation of duties. The right to receive payment is freely assignable; only the duty to paint (personal service) may be non-delegable.
Wrong (D): First Bank as assignee receives only the right to payment, not the obligation to perform the painting services.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2459;

-- Q2460: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under UCC Section 2-601, the perfect tender rule requires that goods delivered under a sale-of-goods contract conform exactly to the contract specifications. Delivering only 80 of 100 contracted widgets is a material breach that entitles Harper to reject the nonconforming goods, suspend performance, and sue for damages.
Wrong (A): A 20% shortfall in quantity exceeds any reasonable de minimis deviation and constitutes a material breach, not a minor one.
Wrong (B): Substantial performance is a common law doctrine for service contracts; the UCC perfect tender rule applies to goods and requires exact conformity.
Wrong (C): Anticipatory repudiation occurs when a party indicates in advance that it will not perform; Garcia actually delivered, though only partially.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2460;

-- Q2461: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 302, an intended third-party beneficiary may enforce the contract against the promisor once the beneficiary''s rights have vested. Rights vest when the beneficiary learns of and assents to the contract, materially changes position in reliance, or brings suit.
Wrong (B): A court order is not required for a third-party beneficiary to have rights. Vesting occurs through knowledge, reliance, or suit — not judicial action.
Wrong (C): An intended third-party beneficiary can enforce the contract even though they are not a party to the agreement, unlike an incidental beneficiary.
Wrong (D): An intended beneficiary need not provide consideration to the promisor; the consideration between the original contracting parties supports the beneficiary''s rights.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2461;

-- Q2462: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UCC Section 2-601, the perfect tender rule allows a buyer to reject the whole, accept the whole, or accept any commercial units and reject the rest if the goods or their delivery fail to conform to the contract in any respect. This is stricter than the common law substantial performance doctrine.
Wrong (B): The perfect tender rule does not require a materiality threshold or a 10% value reduction. Any nonconformity, however minor, permits rejection.
Wrong (C): No written inspection report or 24-hour deadline is required. The buyer must reject within a reasonable time after delivery and seasonably notify the seller.
Wrong (D): The buyer''s right to reject does not depend on whether the nonconformity was detectable by reasonable inspection.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2462;

-- Q2464: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under UCC Section 2-205, a firm offer by a merchant made in a signed writing that assures the offer will be held open is irrevocable for the stated period (up to 3 months) without consideration. This is an exception to the common law rule requiring consideration for option contracts.
Wrong (A): A deposit is not required. UCC Section 2-205 specifically eliminates the consideration requirement for a merchant''s firm offer in a signed writing.
Wrong (B): While the common law requires consideration to keep an offer open, UCC Section 2-205 creates an exception for merchants'' signed written offers.
Wrong (C): There is no automatic 5-business-day revocation rule. The offer remains open for the stated period, up to a maximum of 3 months.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2464;

-- Q2465: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 14, a minor (person under 18) has the power to disaffirm contracts for non-necessities at any time during minority or within a reasonable time after reaching the age of majority. Jones, at 16, may void the $2,000 contract for a non-essential item.
Wrong (A): Partial performance does not bar a minor''s right to disaffirm. The power to void the contract exists regardless of performance status.
Wrong (C): A minor may disaffirm during minority — waiting until the age of majority is not required, though the right also extends for a reasonable time afterward.
Wrong (D): The contract is voidable, not void. It remains enforceable until the minor exercises the right to disaffirm.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2465;

