-- Q2479: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the common law pre-existing duty rule in the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 73, a modification to an existing contract requires new consideration from both parties. Performing or promising an existing obligation is not valid consideration for a new promise.
Wrong (B): Modifications are not automatically valid under common law. Without new consideration, the modification is unenforceable. UCC Section 2-209 eliminates this requirement for goods, but common law applies here.
Wrong (C): While the Statute of Frauds requires certain contracts to be in writing, common law modifications fail for lack of consideration, not merely for lack of a writing.
Wrong (D): Price reductions are not automatically void. A modification reducing the price is enforceable if supported by new consideration from both parties.$EXPL$
