-- Q2480: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 1.01, an agency relationship is created when one person (the principal) manifests assent that another (the agent) shall act on the principal''s behalf and subject to the principal''s control. Mutual consent, benefit, and control are the essential elements.
Wrong (B): A partnership agreement creates co-ownership, not an agency relationship. Partnerships are governed by separate statutes such as the Uniform Partnership Act.
Wrong (C): An employee hired with no supervision lacks the control element essential to agency. The principal''s right to control the agent''s conduct is a defining feature.
Wrong (D): A buyer-seller transaction is a sales contract, not an agency relationship. No one is acting on behalf of or subject to the control of the other.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2480;

-- Q2481: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.01, express authority arises when the principal makes an actual, explicit communication — oral or written — granting the agent specific authority to act. Caldwell''s written agreement directly confers this authority on Fletcher.
Wrong (A): When an agent assumes authority based on circumstances, that describes implied authority under Section 2.01, not express authority.
Wrong (B): When a third party reasonably believes the agent has authority based on the principal''s conduct, that describes apparent authority under Section 2.03.
Wrong (C): An agent acting in an emergency to protect the principal''s interests describes authority by necessity or emergency, not express authority.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2481;

-- Q2482: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.02, implied authority includes the power to take actions that are incidental to, customary in, or reasonably necessary for carrying out the agent''s express authority. Arranging shipping as a purchasing agent is customary for the role.
Wrong (A): Implied authority is limited to acts reasonably necessary and customary, not any action the agent believes would benefit the principal.
Wrong (C): Implied authority does not extend to contracts that exceed the scope of express authorization. Such acts would be unauthorized.
Wrong (D): An agent generally may not delegate all responsibilities to a subagent without the principal''s consent, as this exceeds implied authority.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2482;

-- Q2483: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.03, apparent authority exists when a third party reasonably believes the agent has authority based on the principal''s manifestations — such as granting a title, providing business cards, and allowing the agent to represent the company at trade shows.
Wrong (A): If actual authority has not been communicated to third parties, that is simply undisclosed actual authority, not apparent authority.
Wrong (B): An agent who claims authority without any basis from the principal''s conduct is acting without authority, not under apparent authority.
Wrong (C): An agent exceeding express authority in an emergency may invoke emergency authority, but that is distinct from apparent authority created by the principal''s conduct.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2483;

-- Q2485: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 8.01, a gratuitous agent owes the same fiduciary duties as a compensated agent, including loyalty, obedience, care, accounting, and notification. The acceptance of the agency relationship — not compensation — triggers these obligations.
Wrong (A): The absence of consideration does not eliminate fiduciary duties. Once the agency relationship is accepted, full duties attach regardless of compensation.
Wrong (B): A gratuitous agent owes both the duty of loyalty and the duty of care, not loyalty alone. All fiduciary duties apply.
Wrong (D): Fiduciary duties arise from the agency relationship itself and are not limited to what is expressly stated in writing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2485;

-- Q2486: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.04, the doctrine of respondeat superior holds an employer vicariously liable for torts committed by an employee acting within the scope of employment. Liability attaches even if the employer did not authorize the specific tortious act.
Wrong (A): The employer is not liable for all actions regardless of when or where they occur. Liability is limited to acts within the scope of employment.
Wrong (B): Respondeat superior applies to tortious conduct, not exclusively to criminal acts. Criminal liability of the employer requires separate analysis.
Wrong (C): Respondeat superior covers torts, not only contractual obligations. Contractual liability of the principal arises under different agency principles.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2486;

-- Q2487: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 6.01, when an agent acts for a disclosed principal whose identity is known to the third party, the principal is the real party in interest. The third party''s contractual remedies run against the principal, not the agent.
Wrong (A): The agent who signed on behalf of a disclosed principal is not personally liable. The principal, not the agent, is bound to the contract.
Wrong (B): Joint liability does not apply when the principal is disclosed. Only the principal is liable unless the agent personally guaranteed performance.
Wrong (D): The third party does not assume the risk of nonperformance. The disclosed principal is bound and the third party has full contractual remedies.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2487;

-- Q2488: Agency and Regulation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 6.03, when an agent acts for an undisclosed principal, the third party may hold either the agent or the principal liable once the principal''s identity is discovered. The agent remains liable because the third party relied on the agent''s personal credit.
Wrong (A): The third party does have recourse. Once the undisclosed principal''s identity is discovered, the third party may pursue either the agent or the principal.
Wrong (B): While the agent is liable, the undisclosed principal is also liable once identified. The third party is not limited to recovering solely from the agent.
Wrong (D): The contract is not automatically void. An undisclosed principal arrangement is valid, and the contract remains enforceable against both the agent and the principal.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2488;

-- Q2489: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 8.01, the duty of loyalty requires an agent to disclose all material information, avoid self-dealing, and not compete with the principal during the agency. Mitchell must disclose the developer''s above-market offer and refrain from purchasing for personal profit.
Wrong (B): The duty of loyalty does not require working exclusively for one principal. An agent may serve multiple principals if there is no conflict of interest and proper disclosure is made.
Wrong (C): The duty of loyalty involves acting in the principal''s best interests, not passively accepting any compensation. It imposes affirmative obligations of disclosure and avoidance of conflicts.
Wrong (D): No court order is required before entering transactions on the principal''s behalf. The agent must act within authority and disclose material facts, but judicial approval is not a prerequisite.$EXPL$
